<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>New distortion and a central blurred spot in the left eye of an 80-year-old painter, suspicious for neovascular age-related macular degeneration. Please evaluate this week and treat as indicated. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within the week, because of possible choroidal neovascularization. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is this neovascular age-related macular degeneration, and should anti-VEGF treatment start now?</item><item>What is the state of the right eye, and should she take an AREDS2 supplement to protect it?</item><item>How should she monitor each eye at home, and what should prompt a same-day call?</item><item>Would low-vision rehabilitation help her keep painting, and is she safe to continue driving short daytime routes?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>A few weeks of straight lines looking bent and a gray central spot in the left eye, with trouble reading small print and judging fine color differences. Amsler grid today shows wavy lines and a small central gray patch in the left eye and a normal grid in the right. Best-corrected acuity 78 ETDRS letters right and 56 left. No eye pain, redness, flashes, or floaters. She takes no medicines, including no blood thinners, and has no known allergies. PHQ-2 1 and Mini-Cog 5 of 5. She paints daily, lives alone, and drives short routes by day [S]</paragraph><list><item>Afraid of losing the central vision she needs to paint. [S]</item><item>Wants to keep living alone and driving to her studio. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Georgia Totto O&apos;Keeffe was born on November 15, 1887, to Francis Calyxtus O&apos;Keeffe and Ida Totto O&apos;Keeffe at the family dairy farm near Sun Prairie, Wisconsin, the second of seven children. [D]</item><item>O&apos;Keeffe&apos;s mother died in Charlottesville, Virginia, in May 1916, and her father died in Petersburg, Virginia, in November 1918. [D]</item><item>In 1927 O&apos;Keeffe was admitted twice to Mount Sinai Hospital in New York for surgery to remove benign breast cysts. [D]</item><item>In February 1933 O&apos;Keeffe was admitted to Doctor&apos;s Hospital in New York, suffering from psychoneurosis, and recuperated in Bermuda in March and April. [D]</item><item>O&apos;Keeffe&apos;s eyesight began to fail in 1968. [D]</item><item>The first signs of O&apos;Keeffe&apos;s macular degeneration may have appeared as early as 1964. [R]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

